1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,779
When a user loses the phone that holds
their authenticator,

2
00:00:04,779 --> 00:00:09,131
reset their one-time password: delete the
old authenticator,

3
00:00:09,131 --> 00:00:11,742
then make the user set up a new one.

4
00:00:11,742 --> 00:00:13,450
This is Keycloak 26.8.2.

5
00:00:14,633 --> 00:00:19,544
Before you start, confirm that the request
comes from the user,

6
00:00:19,544 --> 00:00:23,598
through a contact you had before the phone
was lost.

7
00:00:23,598 --> 00:00:30,033
Then sign in to the admin console with an
account that can manage the realm’s users.

8
00:00:31,233 --> 00:00:34,864
Check that the user’s realm is the current
realm.

9
00:00:34,864 --> 00:00:39,293
Select Users, search for the user, and
select their username.

10
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:50,393
Select the Credentials tab.

11
00:00:50,393 --> 00:00:53,444
The row of type OTP is the authenticator,

12
00:00:53,444 --> 00:00:56,793
and its label names the device: Alex’s
phone.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,160
Open the row’s menu, and select Delete.

14
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:06,528
Confirm with Delete.

15
00:01:06,528 --> 00:01:08,503
Only the password is left.

16
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:11,802
Don’t stop here.

17
00:01:11,802 --> 00:01:18,530
The default sign-in flow asks for a code
only from a user who has an authenticator,

18
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:24,203
so until the next step, this user can sign
in with the password alone.

19
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,021
Select the Details tab.

20
00:01:27,021 --> 00:01:30,262
In Required user actions, choose Configure
OTP.

21
00:01:30,262 --> 00:01:31,410
Then select Save.

22
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,720
At the next sign-in, after the password,

23
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:46,744
Keycloak asks the user to set up an
authenticator.

24
00:01:46,744 --> 00:01:52,940
They scan the QR code with the app on
their new phone, type the code it shows,

25
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:55,720
name the device, and select Submit.

26
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:06,771
To check, open the Credentials tab again.

27
00:02:06,771 --> 00:02:11,349
The new OTP row has the new device’s name
and today’s date.

28
00:02:11,349 --> 00:02:14,477
And the admin events record the deletion.

29
00:02:21,467 --> 00:02:27,593
The written guide has every step, and what
to do when a code is rejected.

30
00:02:27,593 --> 00:02:31,567
Find it in the library at
karandeshmukh.dev.
